      <memberdef kind="function" id="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-utility-functions_1gaa2f0f4cff320a783ad2347c9ff3fc731" prot="public" static="no" nodiscard="yes" const="no" explicit="no" inline="no" virt="non-virtual">
        <type>bool</type>
        <definition>bool micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::utility::is_match</definition>
        <argsstring>(std::string_view input, std::string_view pattern)</argsstring>
        <name>is_match</name>
        <qualifiedname>micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus::utility::is_match</qualifiedname>
        <param>
          <type>std::string_view</type>
          <declname>input</declname>
        </param>
        <param>
          <type>std::string_view</type>
          <declname>pattern</declname>
        </param>
        <briefdescription>
<para>Checks whether a string matches a pattern with <computeroutput>*</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>?</computeroutput> wildcards. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>input</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The string view to be checked. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>pattern</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The string view containing the pattern to match. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para><computeroutput>true</computeroutput> if the input string matches the pattern; otherwise, <computeroutput>false</computeroutput>.</para>
</simplesect>
This function enables pattern-based string comparison for tests, supporting both exact matches and wildcard patterns. The pattern may include <computeroutput>*</computeroutput> to match any sequence of characters (including an empty one) and <computeroutput>?</computeroutput> to match any single character. This allows for flexible validation of string content in test assertions, accommodating variable or partially known values.</para>
<para>The match is iterative, with no recursion, so the stack usage is constant, which matters on embedded targets. When a mismatch occurs after a <computeroutput>*</computeroutput>, only the most recent <computeroutput>*</computeroutput> is retried, one character further in the input; the earlier ones never need to be, since any match they could produce is also found by the most recent one. The worst-case complexity is <computeroutput>O(n * m)</computeroutput>, for an input of length <computeroutput>n</computeroutput> and a pattern of length <computeroutput>m</computeroutput>.</para>
<para>The function is also used by the runner to select the suites and the test cases matching the <computeroutput>--filter</computeroutput> patterns, for each of the alternatives separated by <computeroutput>|</computeroutput>.</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Examples</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="keyword">namespace<sp/></highlight><highlight class="normal">mt<sp/>=<sp/><ref refid="namespacemicro__os__plus_1_1micro__test__plus" kindref="compound">micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus</ref>;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"></highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">t.expect<sp/>(mt::utility::is_match<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;abc&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;a?c&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;abc<sp/>matches<sp/>a?c&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">t.expect<sp/>(mt::utility::is_match<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;abc&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;a*c&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;abc<sp/>matches<sp/>a*c&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting> </para>
        </detaileddescription>
